 "poem": "The damsel of the Isaurian mountain\nHer youth is golden, and her youth appears\nThe first bliss to all by education won;\nBut she in vain would urge that beauty\nTo higher degrees: I think again\nShe hath not lost a length; though hitherto\nNo palace had been of its maintain\nFor her who, unhappy but truly,\nA vast multitude in some destination\nMust leave them at each other's command.\nYet how little, without further duration,\nThis flower grew on old Fortuny's hand,",
